  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nNow-Tonight...Cumulus continues to build northward this afternoon, \nfiltered by plenty of sunshine. Scattered showers and storms are \nwell under way, emanating from the Space and Treasure Coasts, \ngradually shifting westward. The east coast breeze and individual \noutflow boundaries will help push convection inland through the \nafternoon and early evening hours. Storm motions will be erratic at \ntimes, and gusty winds (40 to 50 mph) may accompany the stronger \nstorms. The overall risk for flooding is low, though poorly-drained \nand low-lying areas could briefly experience ponding of water where \nrain rates are high (15z XMR PW 2.33\"). \n\nBased on radar and guidance trends, a sea breeze collision \nnear/just west of the Orlando metro is likely after 5 PM, \npotentially making for a stormy evening commute. There could be \nsome push back of rain/storms toward the coast (models are hinting\nat Melbourne southward) prior to dissipating around 10-11 PM. \nOvernight, rain and storms will stay focused over the Atlantic, \nwith some activity brushing the Treasure Coast closer to daybreak \nSaturday. Temperatures remain mild tonight, ranging through the \n70s.\n\nSaturday-Sunday...Little change to the overall pattern is forecast \nthis weekend as an upper level ridge axis stretches E-W across the \nstate. Each day may start with isolated showers or a few storms \noffshore, approaching the Treasure/southern Space Coasts closer to \ndaybreak. Light SE winds will be locally enhanced along the east \ncoast breeze each afternoon as it drifts inland, sparking scattered \nshowers and lightning storms. Subtle differences in CAM guidance may \nalter the exact timing/location of an afternoon/evening sea breeze \ncollision each day. However, onshore flow both days favors a \ncollision across interior locations. A strong storm or two cannot\nbe ruled out, producing gusty, water-loaded downdraft winds at \ntimes. Temperature trends remain similar both days, reaching the \nlow 90s in the afternoon and retreating into the 70s overnight. It\nwill continue to feel quite muggy out there, and heat index \nvalues 100-106 degrees are forecast. Stay cool and hydrated, \ntaking breaks out of the heat!\n\nMonday-Friday...There are some changes on the way next week, moreso \nfrom Wednesday onward, as an upper ridge axis weakens over central \nFlorida. The northern jet stream becomes more progressive to start \nthe week with an H5 high building across the central and southern \nU.S. Monday-Tuesday. Tuesday into early Wednesday, models indicate \nupper level low pressure builds south and west along the Mid \nAtlantic/Carolina coast, eventually reaching this part of the state \nlater Wednesday into Thursday. There is some uncertainty, namely if \nthe elongated low pressure can organize into an upper low by \nWednesday. Into Thursday and Friday, H5 low pressure retrogrades \ntoward the Gulf underneath weak ridging to the north. \n\nSurface high pressure expands southward on Tuesday and Wednesday, \ntightening the pressure gradient a bit. Monday's light onshore \nflow will strengthen in response by Tuesday and Wednesday, with \nNBE probabilities of 20+ mph winds around 30-40% at the coast \nTuesday, expanding inland (40-50%+) Wednesday. There are still \nsome glaring differences in available moisture advertised between \nthe models beyond Tuesday, so this forecast maintains rain chances\nnear or slightly above climatology. With the potential for an \nupper low overhead or nearby, and increased onshore flow, \nscattered showers and occasional storms look to continue next \nweek. Above-normal temperatures settle closer to normals beyond \nMonday, with the exception of warmer overnight lows closer to the \ncoast.",
